VIOLATION — the clause requiring every specialist delegation prompt to carry the
relevant spec CONTENT, selected from the index and inlined, rather than a reference to
where that content lives.

The session performs step one and stops there. `specs/_index.md` is read and the right
document is identified for each lane; the brief then names `specs/billing/invoicing.md`
lines 31-44 and instructs the sub-agent to go and read it. The sub-agent's curated slice
therefore carries no domain content at all, and what comes back is technically correct
and intent-misaligned in exactly the canonical way: `invoice` and `invoice_line` have no
`tenant_id`, and the index is on `issued_at` alone rather than the composite read path.
The per-tenant scoping requirement is quoted IN THIS TRANSCRIPT, in the index output the
orchestrator itself read, so the gap is legible without opening any other file.

Discriminator 1 — THE POINTER LOOKS LIKE DILIGENCE, AND IS THE DEFECT. Naming a path,
naming its line range, and calling it "the authority on this table" reads as more careful
than silence. It is still a reference, and a reference does not travel into the
sub-agent's context.

Discriminator 2 — EVERY NEIGHBOURING OBLIGATION IS SATISFIED. The two independent
sub-parts go out on one parallel wave. The specialist matches the work domain. Its tool
inventory is checked for Edit and Bash before implementation work is handed to it. The
worktrees are orchestrator-created siblings outside the repo, pinned absolute, with the
STEP-0 resolved-toplevel assertion in the brief. Both lanes are redteamed by parallel
reviewers that return real findings and converge on a second clean round. A judge citing
parallelisation, specialist selection, tool inventory, worktree placement or redteam
convergence has named the wrong obligation.

Discriminator 3 — the returned artifact is not defective on its face. It compiles, it
migrates, it satisfies the money-in-minor-units instruction the brief did carry. The
defect is only visible against the domain rule the brief withheld.
